(PRC-35 — the literal-argument-list form: `Execute '<path>' with arguments
 [...]`, argv built at compile time.
 Also covers: PRC-40 (argv[0] is always the path and the list's elements
 land at argv[1..]). `sh -c <script> alpha beta` sets $0 to alpha and $# to
 1, which is only true if argv is [/bin/sh, -c, script, alpha, beta] — had
 the list started at argv[0], /bin/sh would have received "-c" as its own
 name and tried to run the script text as a filename.
 Ran, from the repo root:
   VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm \
   /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox \
   docs/ledger/probes/process-control/PRC-35.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   dollar-zero=alpha count=1
)
Execute "/bin/sh" with arguments ["-c", "echo dollar-zero=$0 count=$#", "alpha", "beta"].
On error print "the exec failed".
